# Hero Story Azure App Service Deployment Plan

## Goal

Prepare and deploy Hero Story to Azure using Azure App Service for the ASP.NET Core API and its separately scalable image-generation worker. Use managed Azure services for persistence, queueing, and blob storage, and deploy the Vue single-page application as a static site. Keep the existing OpenAI integration unless a separate provider migration is approved.

This document is the work plan, not a record that any Azure resources have been provisioned. Delivery status belongs in [the roadmap](../docs/roadmap.md).

## Project Information

- **Application:** Hero Story, an authenticated story-generation application.
- **Frontend:** Vue 3, TypeScript, Vite, Pinia, and Vue Router.
- **API:** ASP.NET Core 10 Web API with JWT authentication and EF Core.
- **Worker:** .NET 10 background service polling Azure Queue Storage for image-generation jobs.
- **Data and media:** SQL Server-compatible persistence, Azure Queue Storage, and Azure Blob Storage.
- **External provider:** OpenAI for text generation, moderation, and the configured image-generation strategy.
- **Local development:** Docker Compose provides SQL Server and Azurite only; it is not the production deployment topology.

## Recommended Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Reader[Reader browser] --> Frontend[Azure Static Web Apps<br/>Vue SPA]
    Frontend -->|HTTPS / bearer token| Api[Azure App Service<br/>ASP.NET Core API]
    Api --> Sql[Azure SQL Database]
    Api --> Queue[Azure Queue Storage]
    Api --> Blob[Azure Blob Storage]
    Worker[Azure App Service<br/>Continuous WebJob worker] --> Queue
    Worker --> Sql
    Worker --> Blob
    Api --> OpenAI[OpenAI API]
    Worker --> OpenAI
    Api -. managed identity .-> KeyVault[Azure Key Vault]
    Worker -. managed identity .-> KeyVault
    Api --> Insights[Application Insights / Log Analytics]
    Worker --> Insights
```

### Hosting decisions

- Host the API as an Azure App Service Web App on a supported .NET 10 runtime. Use HTTPS-only access, deployment slots where the selected plan supports them, and Always On for predictable startup.
- Host the worker as a continuous WebJob on a separate Windows App Service Web App and App Service plan. Separate plans allow API and worker scaling to be controlled independently. Verify the existing generic-host worker can be packaged and operated as a continuous WebJob before committing to this option. If that proof fails, evaluate a dedicated App Service custom-container worker with an appropriate process-liveness/health strategy.
- Host the compiled Vue SPA on Azure Static Web Apps. Use a separate App Service Web App for the SPA only if consolidating hosting outweighs the static-hosting advantages.
- Use Azure SQL Database for production persistence, Azure Queue Storage for image jobs and poison messages, and Azure Blob Storage for generated artwork and private portraits.
- Use managed identity for Azure resource access, Key Vault for secrets that cannot use identity-based authentication, and Application Insights with a shared Log Analytics workspace for telemetry.
- Keep source portraits in a private blob container, separate from generated artwork. Preserve the existing opaque portrait provenance boundary in queue messages and logs.

Select App Service and database SKUs after agreeing on availability, scale, and workload requirements. This plan intentionally contains no cost estimate.

## Work Packages

### 1. Confirm hosting and release shape

- [ ] Confirm the Azure region, environment separation (at least non-production and production), availability target, and expected traffic/workload.
- [ ] Prove the .NET worker can run as a continuous WebJob, start reliably after app restarts, shut down cleanly, and expose useful logs. Confirm the required App Service OS and runtime support.
- [ ] Confirm separate App Service plans for API and worker to allow independent scaling; document the selected deployment-slot strategy for the API.
- [ ] Confirm Azure Static Web Apps for the SPA, its production API URL, custom-domain/TLS needs, and preview-environment policy.
- [ ] Define scale triggers and limits for the API and worker. The worker currently polls in batches; choose a polling/scale policy that does not create duplicate in-flight image generation or uncontrolled provider usage.

### 2. Make application configuration production-safe

- [ ] Make the API fail startup outside Development when SQL configuration is absent; do not allow production to silently fall back to EF InMemory.
- [ ] Define the production configuration contract for API and worker: SQL, queue and blob endpoints/container names, OpenAI settings, JWT issuer/audience/lifetimes, CORS origins, and queue retry/visibility settings.
- [ ] Remove Azurite defaults from production paths or reject them outside Development. Keep local Development behavior explicit.
- [ ] Replace the production Content Security Policy's localhost API origin with configuration for the deployed frontend/API origins. Configure CORS with the exact frontend origins.
- [ ] Set `VITE_API_BASE_URL` to the deployed API URL at frontend build time; ensure development login is disabled in production builds and remains unavailable outside the Development API environment.
- [ ] Review upload limits, request timeouts, rate limits, token handling, and proxy/forwarded-header behavior for the selected App Service topology.

### 3. Identity, secrets, and network security

- [ ] Enable system-assigned or user-assigned managed identities for the API and worker.
- [ ] Configure Azure SQL Microsoft Entra authentication, create contained database users for the API and worker managed identities, and grant only the required database permissions; Azure RBAC alone does not grant SQL data-plane access.
- [ ] Update SQL, Queue, and Blob SDK construction to support token credentials through managed identity; avoid storage account keys and SQL passwords where supported.
- [ ] Review blob URL generation: private images must remain readable through short-lived, identity-compatible user-delegation SAS URLs or an authenticated API path. Do not return an unsigned private blob URL as though it were a usable signed URL.
- [ ] Store the OpenAI API key and any unavoidable credentials in Key Vault; grant access through managed identity and define rotation ownership. Do not put secrets in source control, frontend variables, deployment logs, or queue payloads.
- [ ] Decide SQL and Storage network exposure. Prefer private endpoints and App Service VNet integration where operationally supported; otherwise restrict public access to the narrowest feasible network boundary.
- [ ] Require TLS, HTTPS-only App Service access, secure storage settings, and production-grade JWT signing material. Review data-plane and management-plane role assignments.

### 4. Provision infrastructure with Bicep

- [ ] Create modular Bicep under `infra/` for resource group/environment parameters, App Service plans and apps, Static Web Apps, SQL, Storage, Key Vault, monitoring, identities, RBAC, and network integration as selected.
- [ ] Parameterize names, region, SKU, environment, origins, and non-secret settings. Keep secrets out of Bicep parameters and deployment outputs.
- [ ] Configure private portrait storage separately from generated artwork; configure queue and poison-queue names consistently with the API and worker.
- [ ] Enable diagnostic settings, retention appropriate to policy, and resource locks/policies where required by the deployment environment.
- [ ] Validate Bicep and review an Azure deployment preview/what-if before provisioning. Keep infrastructure changes separate from application release changes where practical.

### 5. Database and migration rollout

- [ ] Provision Azure SQL Database and configure encrypted connections, backups, access controls, and restore expectations.
- [ ] Establish a deployment-safe EF Core migration process. Generate and review a migration bundle or equivalent one-time release step; do not let multiple API instances race to apply migrations at startup.
- [ ] Disable `DB_APPLY_MIGRATIONS` in production app settings after the release migration step is established.
- [ ] Use expand-and-contract schema changes when API and worker versions can overlap during deployment. Test upgrade and rollback behavior against SQL Server/Azure SQL rather than relying only on EF InMemory tests.
- [ ] Document backup retention, point-in-time restore, recovery objectives, and how restore affects sessions, portraits, generated assets, and queued jobs.

### 6. Build and deployment automation

- [ ] Add GitHub Actions deployment environments for non-production and production with approval protection for production.
- [ ] Authenticate the workflow to Azure using workload identity federation/OIDC; do not use long-lived Azure client secrets.
- [ ] Build and test the .NET solution and Vue frontend in CI, then publish distinct API, worker/WebJob, and frontend artifacts.
- [ ] Deploy infrastructure through reviewed Bicep changes and deploy application artifacts through repeatable release jobs.
- [ ] Order releases so infrastructure and compatible database migrations complete before API/worker rollout; deploy the frontend with its production API base URL.
- [ ] Use API deployment slots and health-based swap/rollback where supported. Define worker update behavior so only the intended worker version consumes queue messages during rollout.
- [ ] Ensure deployment logs and artifacts do not expose secrets, portrait data, tokens, or sensitive prompts.

### 7. Health, observability, and operations

- [ ] Add API liveness/readiness endpoints and configure App Service health checks. Make readiness reflect required dependencies without exposing credentials or internal error details.
- [ ] Define a worker liveness signal and alert on stopped/restarting WebJobs, queue backlog/age, poison messages, repeated job failures, and unhealthy dependencies.
- [ ] Add structured logs, correlation IDs across API-to-worker jobs, distributed tracing where practical, and metrics for request latency, generation latency, moderation outcomes, queue age, retries, and provider failures.
- [ ] Connect API and worker to Application Insights and a shared Log Analytics workspace with appropriate sampling and retention.
- [ ] Define alerts, dashboards, incident ownership, poison-queue inspection/replay procedures, and operational runbooks.
- [ ] Review log fields to ensure no secrets, bearer tokens, source portrait bytes/URLs, or unnecessary user story content are captured.

### 8. Product, privacy, and launch gates

- [ ] Resolve the production policy decisions tracked in [the roadmap](../docs/roadmap.md): age bands/content ratings, mature themes, irreversible outcomes, sharing, retention/export, and minor access to hero-likeness personalization.
- [ ] Document consent scope, provider scope, portrait retention/deletion, generated-art retention, backup expiry, and account/session deletion behavior for the selected Azure services and OpenAI provider.
- [ ] Confirm provider terms and data handling for text, moderation, image generation, and likeness references. Azure hosting does not itself change the OpenAI integration or provider-retention behavior.
- [ ] Test authentication and ownership boundaries, CORS/CSP, moderation failures, SQL/storage/provider outages, queue redelivery and poison handling, portrait deletion during queued work, and backup restore.
- [ ] Complete a production security review and verify data access, secret rotation, recovery, and incident response before public launch.

## Completion Criteria

- [ ] Infrastructure is reproducible from reviewed Bicep and deployment configuration.
- [ ] API and worker run on separate App Service scaling boundaries; the Vue SPA is served over HTTPS and calls only the configured API origin.
- [ ] SQL, Queue, and Blob access use least-privilege identities where supported, and private portrait blobs are not publicly accessible.
- [ ] Database migrations execute once through the release process, with documented rollback/recovery handling.
- [ ] Health signals, telemetry, alerts, and operational runbooks cover API, worker, database, storage, queue, and external-provider failure modes.
- [ ] Product, privacy, and security launch gates are resolved and verified against the deployed environment.

## Execution Order

1. Confirm hosting shape and prove continuous WebJob operation.
2. Harden production configuration and identity-based SQL/Storage access.
3. Establish schema rollout, health checks, and observability.
4. Create and review Bicep infrastructure.
5. Add OIDC-based CI/CD and deploy to non-production.
6. Run end-to-end, security, recovery, and product-policy checks.
7. Approve production launch and deploy.